Replying to your question as to whether, under this Bill, the association has a power to do a banking or loan business, or to engage in retail or wholesale business, or to create a monopoly of any kind.
Mr. Chairman, I appeal to you to keep order. I am reading the opinion of the law clerk of the House of Commons.

House went into committee to consider the following proposed resolution:
Resolved, that it is expedient to continue tor a further period the granting of aid towards the maintenance of an independent and efficient service of telegraphic news from Great Britain for publication in the Cana-Sir FREDERICK BORDEN
dian press, and that for such purpose it is expedient to provide for the payment of the following sums:-
A sum not exceeding $12,000 for the twelve months terminating June 30, 1911;
A sum not exceeding $10,000 for the twelve months terminating June 30, 1912;
A sum not exceeding $8,000 for the twelve months terminating June 30, 1913;
A sum not exceeding $6,000 for the twelve months terminating June 30, 1914;
And a sum not exceeding $5,000 for the twelve months terminating June 30, 1915.
Mr. FIELDING Some years ago the attention of parliament was drawn to the fact that the cable news which came to us from Great Britain was at that time almost entirely prepared for the journals of the neighbouring republic and had very little regard for the news in which Canada was concerned. The desirability of reform in that respect was generally recognized, and with the general consent of the House an arrangement was come to with the leading Canadian newspapers whereby we should give them an annual subsidy to assist, them in obtaining a better cable service, on the basis of contributing dollar for dollar with them for that purpose. That is to say, for every dollar which they paid for cable rates we should contribute a. dollar to assist them, with a certain limitation as to the maximum sum. That arrangement ran for some years and is about to expire. We hope the time will come, and that before long, when our Canadian newspapers will be strong enough to perform this service of themselves. However, they have represented that it is yerv desirable we should give them some further aid, and after consideration we have concluded to ask the House to agree to the resolution which is now presented, which proposes to give them for five years the following sums: $12,000 for the first year, $10,000 for the second year, $8,000 for the third year, $6,000 for the fourth year and $5,000 for the fifth year. When these_ subsidies were originally '/hranted we certainly had the hope that the service would gradually become self-supporting. However, it is repre=ented to us that if the_ subsidy were dropped the service, which, take it all in all, has been a very useful one, might be affected, and .as the object in view is one which I am sure will commend itself to the consideration of the House generally, we have concluded tc- ask the House to give this further aid. The arrangement is made with a combination of journals known as the Canadian Associated Press. A number of the leading. journals of Canada, irrespective of politics, agreed to form an association for that purpose. They arranged for the receiving of the cables, and they in turn distributed them to other journals through-

out Canada which might be willing to receive them. At one time there was some criticism as to the efficiency of the service, and occasional suggestions have been made that the government should interfere with a view to affecting the class of information to be furnished. We have always had a very decided opinion that the government should not .interfere in the matter at all. We said to the newspaper proprietors that if they can agree among themselves to establish an organization for the purpose, taking all responsibility of management, we would be very glad to help them, but the government had no desire to have anything to do with the management or control of the service. Although there is occasional criticism, and I will not say the service has always been satisfactory, it has been the means of giving to Canada a ver.y useful class of information which probably we could not have received but for the making of this arrangement. I am very glad to present the matter to the House with the view of giving the newspapers this further assistance for the period of five years.
The correspondence I have on the subject comes from Mr. J. Ross Robertson of the Toronto 'Telegram,' who is the gentleman most keenlv interested in the matter, and has been from the beginning. He has addressed to me a letter setting forth what he believes to be evidence of the efficiency of the service. I would he very happy to read it.

More than two weeks ago;
I think three weeks ago. It was on the order paper, printed and in the hands of members for a considerable time. It has been the subject of consideration for two or three weeks, and the statement of my hon. friend is not fair in suggesting that there is any attempt to force an unconsidered Bill through the House or in suggesting that there' is a reason for suspecion that there is an ulterior motive in connection with this Bill. The purpose of the Bill has been explained before, and there is no reason why it should not be explain-

ed again if it is necessary, but I beg to assure my hon. friend that there is urgency for the passage of this Bill for the protection of large and material interests. There is nothing concealed in the Bill, and there is no intention of using the Bill to the advantage of one interest or another. It is a common interest that requires protection, and it is the duty of parliament to protect that interest. The government, being aware of that fact, asks parliament to give the necessary authority by means of this legislation. As to whether it is necessary to pass this legislation, the jurisdiction which has heretofore been exercised by the province of British Columbia in Tegard to the water rights in the railway belt has been declared by the Exchequer Court, a decision which has been sustained by the Supreme Court, to be vested in the Dominion of Canada; in other words the water rights which have been granted by British Columbia, have, under that decision, no legal standing at the present time. These water rights involve value to the extent of thousands and perhaps hundreds of thousands of dollars, and I submit that it is not proper to leave these rights unprotected by proper authority. We ask parliament to pass this legislation so that the rights which are now in question as the result of the decision of the Exchequer and Supreme Courts may be confirmed and maintained. That, it seems to me, is a matter of urgency and necessity. There is no infringement of provincial or any other rights and there is no advantage to be gained by one right over another.
